{
    "title": "Protecting American Investors From Foreign-Owned and Politically-Motivated Proxy Advisors",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 16, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-16/pdf/2025-23093.pdf",
    "summary": "The order directs federal agencies to increase oversight of two major proxy advisory firms and to curb what it describes as politically driven influence on how big investors vote in corporate elections. It asks regulators to focus on transparency, conflicts of interest, and whether advice that prioritizes social or environmental goals over financial returns is appropriate.\n\nIf this moves forward as described, everyday savers could benefit\u2014especially people with workplace retirement plans and personal retirement accounts\u2014through tighter standards meant to keep voting advice focused on financial results. Large companies may also welcome fewer outside pressures on issues like board diversity and climate targets, and fund managers could get clearer rules on how they use outside advisers. Supporters argue that more disclosure, tougher anti-fraud enforcement, and possible registration for these firms will reduce conflicts and improve the quality of recommendations.\n\nThose who might be negatively affected include the proxy advisory firms themselves, which could face new registration, compliance costs, and limits on how they incorporate environmental, social, and governance considerations. Shareholder advocates and investors who want these factors weighed in corporate decision-making may see fewer such proposals succeed, and pension funds that rely on independent research could find it harder or more expensive to get it. Critics warn the changes could narrow shareholder rights, chill legitimate research, and reduce the tools investors use to manage long-term risks.\n\nIt\u2019s also important to note that this order does not change rules on its own. The Securities and Exchange Commission, the Federal Trade Commission, and the Department of Labor must go through public processes to revise guidance and regulations. That means any shifts will take time, could be modified after public comment, and may face legal challenges.",
